// Congruence, form 1 of 2: a VALUE CONTEXT (ADR-76). From `p : Path(Integer,
// x, y)` derive a proof that the lists `[x]` and `[y]` are equal -- one `walk`,
// with the value context `V[-] = [-]` applied to both endpoints inside the
// motive.
//
// This is one of exactly TWO honest congruence forms in CBPV. General
// congruence -- `Path(B, f(x), f(y))` for an arbitrary function `f` -- is NOT
// derivable: `f(x)` is a COMPUTATION, and identity is a type of VALUES; an
// effectful `f` has no value-level image to compare. What IS derivable:
// congruence under a value constructor context (this file), and congruence of
// the THUNKS of the two applications (`cong-thunk.gandr`).
//
// The motive here is the walk-form's own identity former `Path(?, [x], [y])`:
// the motive is part of walk's binder syntax (not a general type position), so
// its endpoints may be COMPOUND value terms -- exactly what congruence needs.
// The `?` carrier is the gradual unknown; conversion's consistency lets the
// base's precisely-typed `here` inhabit it. The base ascribes its witness
// (`[x] : List(Integer)`) because list literals are check-only.

def cong_list = thunk {
  fn(p: Path(Integer, 4, 4)) {
    walk(p,
      fn(x, y, q) { Path(?, [x], [y]) },
      fn(x) { ret here(([x] : List(Integer))) })
  }
};

cong_list(here(4))

// The harness checks:
//@ expect: clean
//@ expect-def: cong_list
//@ expect-last-value: here([4])
